course: dictionary
lesson: 958
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: One small word can have many meanings, and blow is a good example. The words around it give the clues. «The wind is blowing» means air is moving. «Blow out the candles» means pushing air out of your mouth.
explain: Check the other words in the sentence. «The leaves blew away» is about the wind moving leaves, not about anyone's mouth. «Losing was a blow» uses blow in a different way: here it means a sudden shock or disappointment.
explain: Ask yourself: what would fit here? Then swap your meaning into the sentence and read it again. If it still makes sense, you probably chose well.
fact: Blow can be a verb or a noun. As a verb its forms are blow, blows, blew, blown and blowing.
fact: «Blow» can mean to move, as in «The wind is blowing».
fact: «Blow» can mean to push air out of your mouth, as in «blow out candles» or «blow your nose».
fact: «Blow up» can mean to fill with air, like a balloon, or to explode.
fact: A «blow» can be a hard hit, or a sudden shock, as in «Losing was a blow».
exercise: «The wind is blowing hard tonight.» What does blow mean here? | To sing a loud note | To hit someone with a hand | *To move, as air does in the wind | To push air out of your mouth
exercise: «Blow out the candles before you eat the cake.» What does blow out mean? | *To push air out of your mouth | To fill with air | To cry | To hit the cake
exercise: «Losing the match was a blow.» What does blow mean here? | A whistle | A soft breeze | *A sudden shock or disappointment | A bubble
test: «The leaves blew away in the autumn wind.» What does blew mean here? | *Moved by the wind | Took a deep breath | Exploded | Hit hard
test: «Blowing on the hot soup cools it down.» What does blowing do to the soup? | Pushes it up | Makes it hotter | Turns it into bubbles | *Makes it cooler
exam: «The wolf huffed and puffed and blew the house down.» Which meaning fits blew? | Hit with a fist | Filled with air, like a balloon | Pushed out through the mouth, like a whistle | *Knocked over by a rush of air
